## Changes

**`webserver/webserver/src/main/java/io/helidon/webserver/ServerListener.java`**
- The idle timeout handler is no longer created and started as a throwaway local in the constructor. The listener now retains the `Timer` (`idleConnectionTimer` field) and the task itself (`volatile IdleTimeoutHandler idleTimeoutHandler` field).
- `startIdleTimeoutTask()` — called from `startIt()` (so it runs on both `start()` and `resume()`), creates a fresh `IdleTimeoutHandler` each time, since a cancelled `TimerTask` cannot be rescheduled.
- `cancelIdleTimeoutTask()` — idempotent cleanup (null-check, null the field, then `cancel()`), wired into all three teardown paths:
  - `stopResources()` (listener stop)
  - `suspendForCheckpoint()` (checkpoint suspend)
  - `rollbackFailedStart()` (failed-start rollback, via `suppressCleanupFailure`)
- Added a small test seam `idleTimeoutTaskScheduled()`, following the existing `serverThreadState()` pattern.

**`webserver/webserver/src/test/java/io/helidon/webserver/ServerListenerIdleTimeoutTest.java`** (new)

Constructs `ServerListener` directly with a test-owned `Timer` (the constructor already accepts one — no transport refactoring needed) and uses `Timer.purge()` as the observation point for actual cancellation on the timer queue, so there are no real-time waits (all 3 tests finish in ~0.4s):
- `stopCancelsIdleTimeoutTask` — task not scheduled before start, scheduled by start, cancelled by stop, repeated stop is safe and cancels nothing extra.
- `suspendCancelsIdleTimeoutTaskAndResumeReschedulesIt` — suspend cancels the task, resume schedules a new one, stop cancels the rescheduled task.
- `stopAfterSuspendRemainsSafeWithAlreadyCancelledTask` — the full stop cleanup path after suspend (which runs `stopResources()` because `inCheckpoint` is set) does not fail on the already-cancelled task.

