## Summary

**Root cause:** In `crates/format/src/formatter/expression.rs`, `format_string()` embedded interpolation expressions as regular breakable documents. When a line exceeded the 80-column limit, the pretty-printer broke groups *inside* the interpolation (e.g. splitting call arguments across lines), producing multiline `f"{...}"` interpolations that the lexer rejects with `format_string_multiline_interpolation`.

**Fix:**
- `crates/format/src/lindig.rs` — added `Document::flatten()`, which recursively replaces every `StrictBreak`/`FlexBreak` with its unbroken text so the document renders on one line regardless of the width limit (inherently multi-line content like verbatim text is preserved).
- `crates/format/src/formatter/expression.rs:239` — interpolation expressions are now pushed as `self.expression(e).flatten()`, so they can never be split.

Line-length pressure now resolves *outside* the string instead: when an f-string argument makes a call too long, the call breaks at its argument boundaries while the f-string stays intact (covered by the new `format_string_interpolation_in_call_breaks_at_args_not_inside` test).

**Tests:** 3 new spec tests with snapshots in `tests/spec/format/`, including the ender-pearl example from the report. All 295 format spec tests pass (idempotency checks in the harness also guarantee formatted output re-parses, i.e. is compiler-accepted). Clippy is clean. The remaining suite failures are exclusively `spec::emit`/`spec::build` snapshots that require the Go toolchain (`gofmt`), which isn't installed in this environment — they fail identically on clean HEAD.

