[Bug]: extension-table 3.23 TableView NodeView ignores HTMLAttributes (regression from 3.22.4)
### Affected Packages

@tiptap/extension-table

### Version(s)

3.23.1 (also 3.23.2, 3.23.4)

### Bug Description

## Summary

In `@tiptap/extension-table` 3.23.x, configuring the Table extension with `HTMLAttributes` no longer applies those attributes to the rendered `<table>` element when `resizable: false` (the default). The class/attributes silently disappear from the DOM.

This is a regression from 3.22.4, where the same configuration worked correctly. `TableView` existed byte-identical in 3.22.4, but the Table extension had no `addNodeView()` method, so ProseMirror always fell back to the schema's `renderHTML`. 3.23 added `addNodeView()` that wires the NodeView in, bypassing `renderHTML`.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { Editor } from '@tiptap/core';
import StarterKit from '@tiptap/starter-kit';
import { Table, TableRow, TableHeader, TableCell } from '@tiptap/extension-table';

new Editor({
  extensions: [
    StarterKit,
    Table.configure({
      HTMLAttributes: { class: 'my-custom-table' }
    }),
    TableRow,
    TableHeader,
    TableCell
  ],
  content: '<table><tbody><tr><td>cell</td></tr></tbody></table>'
});
```

Inspect the rendered DOM. The `<table>` element has no `class="my-custom-table"`.

### Browser Used

Chrome

### Code Example URL

https://codesandbox.io/p/devbox/laughing-glade-n4qh4r

### Expected Behavior

The `<table>` element should render with `class="my-custom-table"` (the documented [Table.configure HTMLAttributes](https://tiptap.dev/docs/editor/extensions/nodes/table) behavior), regardless of the `resizable` setting.

A minimal fix would have `TableView` apply `this.options.HTMLAttributes` to the inner `<table>` element in its constructor.

### Additional Context (Optional)

## Root cause

3.23 introduced `addNodeView()` on the Table extension (extension-table `dist/index.js` around line 645):

```js
addNodeView() {
  const isResizable = this.options.resizable && this.editor.isEditable;
  const View = this.options.View;
  if (isResizable || !View) {
    return null;
  }
  return ({ node, view }) => new View(node, this.options.cellMinWidth, view);
}
```

When `resizable` is false (default) and `View` is the default `TableView`, this returns a NodeView. `TableView` builds the DOM manually:

```js
constructor(node, cellMinWidth) {
  this.dom = document.createElement("div");
  this.dom.className = "tableWrapper";
  this.table = this.dom.appendChild(document.createElement("table"));
  if (node.attrs.style) {
    this.table.style.cssText = node.attrs.style;
  }
  this.colgroup = this.table.appendChild(document.createElement("colgroup"));
  updateColumns(node, this.colgroup, this.table, cellMinWidth);
  this.contentDOM = this.table.appendChild(document.createElement("tbody"));
}
```

`TableView` never reads `this.options.HTMLAttributes`. The schema-level `renderHTML` (which does call `mergeAttributes(this.options.HTMLAttributes, HTMLAttributes, ...)`) is bypassed whenever the NodeView is active.

## Workaround

```ts
Table.configure({
  HTMLAttributes: { class: 'my-custom-table' },
  View: null
})
```

This disables the NodeView and falls back to the schema's `renderHTML`. Side effect: loses `TableView`'s in-place `update(node)` column-width sync; a full re-render happens on attribute changes instead. Acceptable for most read-only or non-resizable use cases.

## Related

[#6176](https://github.com/ueberdosis/tiptap/issues/6176) reports a similar symptom on Tiptap 2.11.5 with `resizable: true`. Different mechanism (no `addNodeView` in 2.x), same user-facing impact.

### Dependency Updates

- [x] Yes, I've updated all my dependencies.

## Interface

No new interfaces are introduced.

